The model has an AUC of 0\.76, but the useful answer is: **contact the riskiest 62% of customers** for a simulated profit of about **$81\,015** on this group, while contacting everyone would earn **$60\,325**. That one chart is what a manager remembers. 💡

## 📋 All eight paths at a glance

| Use case | Baseline first | Then try | Watch out for |
|---|---|---|---|
| 📉 Churn | Logistic regression | Gradient boosting | Leakage from "cancellation" fields |
| 📦 Forecasting | Seasonal naive (same week last year) | LightGBM with lags | Random splits on time data |
| 💳 Fraud | Rules + logistic regression | Gradient boosting | Accuracy on 99.8% legit data |
| ⚙️ Maintenance | Threshold on one sensor | Random forest, LSTM | Engines split across train and test |
| 🧩 Segmentation | RFM quantile buckets | k-means, GMM | Segments nobody can act on |
| 🎫 Ticket triage | Keyword rules | TF-IDF + linear, transformer | Personal data in text |
| 🔍 Inspection | Pre-trained CNN features | Anomaly detection | Lighting changes on the line |
| 🛒 Recommendations | Most popular items | Matrix factorisation | Popularity bias, cold start |

: Baselines and traps {#tbl-paths}

::: {.callout-important}
## ⚠️ Always beat a simple baseline first
If gradient boosting cannot clearly beat "same week last year" or "most popular items", the problem is in the data or the framing, not the model.
